WALdemort
=========

WALdemort (waldemort) is a freestanding frontend program for creating disposable
WAL test fixtures.  It uses the current source tree's WAL structures, alignment,
page magic, and CRC32C implementation, and links frontend libpgcommon,
libpgport, and xlogreader.  It does not need a running server.

WARNING: Never use its output as a replacement for production WAL, or place
corrupted fixtures in an archive used by a valuable database.  Even "valid" WAL
is only structurally valid: it is not a bootstrap/checkpoint sequence or a
replacement for WAL generated by a server.  Permuting records is NOT
semantically safe replay.  Always use disposable clusters and archives.

Examples
--------

Generate 32 NOOP records and a terminal SWITCH in a 16 MB segment:

  waldemort --output 000000010000000000000001

Generate records which cross page boundaries, with reproducible contents:

  waldemort --mode valid --records 8 --payload-size 20000 --seed 42 \
    --output 000000010000000000000001

Inspect a generated segment using the matching build's pg_waldump:

  pg_waldump --path . 000000010000000000000001

Generate different genuine XLOG record types in repeating order:

  waldemort --types noop,restore-point --records 12 --seg-size 1 \
    --output 000000010000000000000001

Other corruption and rearrangement examples (all output paths must be new):

  waldemort --mode mixed --records 16 --output mixed.wal
  waldemort --mode headers --output headers.wal
  waldemort --mode garbage --seed 20 --output garbage.wal
  waldemort --mode headers --input original.wal --output bodies.wal
  waldemort --mode bitflip --input original.wal --offset 100 --mask 0x80 \
    --output flipped.wal
  waldemort --mode truncate --input original.wal --length 8192 \
    --output short.wal
  waldemort --mode permute --input original.wal --seed 7 --output shuffled.wal

Options and modes
-----------------

--output PATH is required.  Creation is exclusive: existing files, symlinks,
hard links, and an input/output alias cannot be overwritten.  A failed write
removes the newly created output.  The input is opened read-only.

--mode defaults to valid:

  valid     Generate correct page and record headers, main-data framing,
            previous-record links, and CRC32C checksums.
  mixed     Generate the same framing, then damage every second synthetic
            record's last payload byte (or checksum when there is no payload).
            Checksums are not recomputed after damage.  The first record and
            terminal SWITCH remain valid.
  headers   Randomize page bodies, retaining every page header byte.  Without
            input, first generate the same layout as valid mode, including
            continuation metadata.  With input, preserve even uninitialized
            or damaged existing page headers; only the first long header is
            required to be valid.
  garbage   Fill an entire segment with deterministic random bytes.
  bitflip   Copy input and XOR exactly one byte at --offset by --mask.
  truncate  Copy exactly --length bytes of input (zero is allowed).
  permute   Import complete records, shuffle them using Fisher-Yates, rebuild
            page framing, relink xl_prev, and recompute CRCs.  Add a terminal
            SWITCH.  Existing rmgr IDs, payloads, block references, images,
            transaction IDs, and record information bits are retained.

--input PATH is required for bitflip, truncate, and permute; optional for
headers and garbage; and prohibited for valid and mixed.  The input must be a
regular, complete segment with a compatible first long header.  Segment size,
base LSN, timeline, and system identifier are derived from that header, not
the filename.  With input, --seg-size, --segno, --timeline, --system-id, --prev,
--records, --payload-size, and --types are rejected.

Generation parameters:

  --seg-size MB      Power of two from 1 to 1024 inclusive; default 16.
  --segno N          Segment number, not its byte address; default 1.
  --timeline N       Nonzero 32-bit timeline ID; default 1.
  --system-id N      64-bit system identifier; default 1.
  --prev X/Y         Previous-record LSN in hexadecimal, or 0; default 0.
                     Must precede the first generated record.
  --records N        Exactly N synthetic records followed by one SWITCH.
                     Default 32; range 0..1000000.  Failure, not silent
                     truncation, if the records and SWITCH do not fit.
  --payload-size N   NOOP main-data length; default 32.  Zero is allowed.
                     Maximum is 16 MB minus the record and long data headers.
  --types LIST       Comma-separated repeating list of noop and restore-point,
                     at most 32 entries; default noop.  A restore-point has
                     its genuine fixed-size xl_restore_point payload, a
                     deterministic timestamp, and a NUL-terminated name;
                     --payload-size affects NOOP only.

--prev, --records, --payload-size, and --types are not accepted for garbage.
All generated non-garbage segments have initialized headers on every page,
zero padding, and a final SWITCH.  There are no cross-segment generated records.

Mutation parameters:

  --offset N        Required only for bitflip, zero-based, below input length.
  --mask N          Only for bitflip, decimal or 0x-prefixed hexadecimal,
                    range 1..255; default 1.
  --length N        Required only for truncate, no greater than input length.

--seed N is an unsigned 64-bit decimal seed, default 1.  It controls NOOP data,
random bytes, and permutation.  SplitMix64 arithmetic, byte order for random
data, and unbiased bounded sampling are specified in the implementation, so
results are reproducible for identical options and input on a compatible
build.  Restore-point payloads do not consume random numbers.  All numeric
options except --prev and hexadecimal --mask require unsigned decimal text,
without whitespace or signs.  Duplicate and incompatible options are errors.
--help and --version print information and exit.

Permutation limitations
-----------------------

Page headers, continuation lengths, record bounds, rmgr IDs, previous links,
and CRC32C are checked before importing complete records.  The matching
XLogReader's DecodeXLogRecord also validates the generic record encoding.
Leading continuation data and a trailing cross-segment record cannot be
imported; they are skipped.  The first validated SWITCH terminates import.
An entirely zero, unwritten tail is accepted; corruption elsewhere is an
error, not a request to search ahead for plausible records.  An input with no
complete non-SWITCH records is rejected.  Overwrite-continuation pages are
unsupported and rejected.  No adjacent segments are read.

Memory is bounded: individual imported records are limited to 16 MB, their
combined raw bytes to 64 MB, and their count to 1000000.  Larger inputs fail
with a diagnostic instead of requesting unbounded memory.  Segment output
and mutation use page-sized buffers, even for 1 GB segments.  A reordered set
that cannot fit together with its terminal SWITCH is rejected before output
creation.

Permutation does not interpret rmgr-specific payloads, decompress full-page
images, rewrite embedded LSNs, or preserve transaction dependencies.  It can
move checkpoints, commit records, relation changes, and other operations to
nonsensical positions.  Its purpose is to exercise parsers and recovery error
paths, not to produce an alternative valid history of a database.

WAL files use the native source version, endianness, structure layout, and
build-time page sizes.  Use the same PostgreSQL build to inspect them.  A
different build/version/platform need not produce or accept the same files.
Choose a canonical 24-character WAL filename matching the selected timeline
and segment number when using tools which infer WAL locations from filenames.
Input files must remain unchanged for the duration of the command.
